  "dom_id": "step-q86898",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R\u2216D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](@asset-0@)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={\u222c}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\u2216D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,\u2216\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
